course: dictionary
lesson: 2345
title: The crux of the matter
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The crux is the most important part of a problem, question or argument: the point that everything else depends on. «The crux of the matter is that we don't have enough money.» The money problem is the key point, so that is what must be sorted out first.
explain: Think of a maths problem. It may have lots of numbers, but one step decides the answer. That step is the crux. Find it first, and the rest becomes much easier.
explain: Writers and speakers use the crux to show what really matters. «The crux of the problem» is the one part that causes the trouble. «The crux of the argument» is the main point the people are arguing about.
explain: Crux has other meanings too. In climbing, it is the hardest move on a route. As a star group, it is the Southern Cross. Look at the context to find which meaning is used. When it is about a problem or an argument, it means the most important part.
fact: The crux is the most important or difficult part of a problem, question or argument.
fact: In climbing, the crux is the hardest move or section on a climbing route.
fact: «Crux», the Southern Cross, is a small, bright group of stars in the southern sky.
fact: «The Southern Cross, Crux, is the smallest of the 88 constellations.»
fact: The Southern Cross is shown on the flags of Australia and New Zealand, and it helps people find south at night.
exercise: «The crux of the matter is that we don't have enough money.» What is the crux here? | *The most important point, which is the money problem | The last sentence on the page | A kind of coin | The name of the bank
exercise: Ruby is stuck on a maths problem. She finds the one step that decides the whole answer. What is that step called? | The title | The footnote | *The crux | The sum of all the numbers
exercise: Which word names the star group in this sentence? «The Southern Cross, Crux, is the smallest of the 88 constellations.» | Constellations | *Crux | Flags | Smallest
test: «The crux of the argument» is the main point people disagree about. What does crux mean? | *The most important part | A small star | The loudest voice | A type of rope
test: Which phrase means the hardest move on a climbing route? | The finish line | *The crux | The starting point | The summit view
exam: Sam says the crux of the argument is always the last thing that was said. Is he right? | *No, the crux is the most important point, not necessarily the last thing said | Yes, because it is the longest part of the argument | No, crux only means a type of flag | Yes, the last thing said is always the crux
